{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 6,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.049,
            0.432,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.088,
            0.847,
            0.146
          ],
          "content": "This problem also can be solved using the fact that addition and subtraction are inverse operations. However, we can use the following method that is perhaps easier to visualise."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.146,
            0.847,
            0.202
          ],
          "content": "To retain only the expression to be evaluated on the LHS, we need to remove the term –(–67). We can remove this term by adding (–67) to both sides of the equation."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.203,
            0.709,
            0.222
          ],
          "content": "12345-5432+132-24-(-67)+(-67)=7091+(-67)",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.222,
            0.709,
            0.241
          ],
          "content": "12345-5432+132-24-(-6\\mathcal{J})+(-6\\mathcal{J})=7091+(-67)",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.242,
            0.51,
            0.26
          ],
          "content": "Thus, $12345-5432+132-24=7024.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.272,
            0.847,
            0.347
          ],
          "content": "? **Example 4:** It is known that $\\left( { \\cfrac { 3 5 } { 1 1 3 } } \\right) \\times 2 4 \\times 1 4 \\times \\left( { \\cfrac { 8 } { 9 } } \\right) = { \\cfrac { 9 4 0 8 0 } { 1 0 1 7 } }$ . What is the value of the expression $\\left( { \\cfrac { 3 5 } { 1 1 3 } } \\right) \\times 2 4 \\times 1 4 ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.349,
            0.844,
            0.408
          ],
          "content": "**Solution:** To retain only the expression to be evaluated on the LHS we need to remove the factor $\\left( { \\frac { 8 } { 9 } } \\right)$ . We can do that by dividing both sides by $\\left( { \\frac { 8 } { 9 } } \\right)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.308,
            0.415,
            0.69,
            0.456
          ],
          "content": "\\left[ \\left( \\frac{35}{113} \\right) \\times 24 \\times 14 \\times \\left( \\frac{8}{9} \\right) \\right] \\div \\left( \\frac{8}{9} \\right) = \\frac{94080}{1017} \\div \\left( \\frac{8}{9} \\right)",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.319,
            0.467,
            0.678,
            0.508
          ],
          "content": "\\left( \\frac{35}{113} \\right) \\times 24 \\times 14 \\times \\left( \\frac{8}{9} \\right) \\times \\left( \\frac{9}{8} \\right) = \\frac{94080}{1017} \\times \\left( \\frac{9}{8} \\right)",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.52,
            0.211,
            0.539
          ],
          "content": "Thus,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.316,
            0.551,
            0.546,
            0.589
          ],
          "content": "\\left( \\frac{35}{113} \\right) \\times 24 \\times 14 = \\frac{11760}{113}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.59,
            0.669,
            0.612
          ],
          "content": "Let us use these ideas to solve the equation $5x - 4 = 7.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.616,
            0.846,
            0.653
          ],
          "content": "What can we do so that 5x is on one side and the equality between the LHS and the RHS still holds?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.654,
            0.846,
            0.692
          ],
          "content": "To retain only 5x on the LHS, we need to remove the term – 4. This can be done by adding 4 to both sides."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.697,
            0.374,
            0.716
          ],
          "content": "Thus, $5x - 4 + 4 = 7 \\pm 4.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.722,
            0.303,
            0.741
          ],
          "content": "Hence, $5x = 11.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.746,
            0.846,
            0.785
          ],
          "content": "To retain only the unknown x on the LHS, we need to remove the factor 5. This can be done by dividing both sides by 5."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.79,
            0.293,
            0.826
          ],
          "content": "Thus, $\\frac { 5 x } { 5 } = \\frac { 1 1 } { 5 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.831,
            0.257,
            0.867
          ],
          "content": "So, $x = { \\frac { 1 1 } { 5 } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.156,
            0.933,
            0.194,
            0.95
          ],
          "content": "170"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
